{
  "query": "MapleStage Inc. runs an online ticketing platform with most customers located in South Korea, and there are no legal requirements that mandate data residency in any specific country or region. You plan to use Cloud SQL for the database. To achieve the lowest latency for your audience, which region should you choose for the database instance?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in asia-southeast1 in Singapore",
      "explanation": "This places the database in Southeast Asia which provides good connectivity within the region",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in a Google Cloud region in South Korea",
      "explanation": "This locates the database near your primary users which typically reduces network latency",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in us-central1 in Iowa because it offers many zones",
      "explanation": "This is a central US region that provides multiple zones for high availability patterns",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in a Google Cloud region in the United States",
      "explanation": "This places the database on the US mainland which may be convenient for teams based in the United States",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Create the instance in a Google Cloud region in South Korea</b>.</p><p>Most users are in South Korea and there are no residency constraints, so placing Cloud SQL in the <b>South Korea region</b> minimizes physical network distance and delivers the lowest latency. The Seoul region, which is asia-northeast3, is closest to your audience and avoids unnecessary interregional network hops that would slow database interactions.</p><p><i>Create the instance in asia-southeast1 in Singapore</i> is geographically farther from South Korea than Seoul, so it introduces extra network distance and would not provide the absolute lowest latency for this user base.</p><p><i>Create the instance in us-central1 in Iowa because it offers many zones</i> is not appropriate for latency because zone count does not compensate for the long geographic distance to Korean users, which would significantly increase round trip time.</p><p><i>Create the instance in a Google Cloud region in the United States</i> would place the database far from most users and would raise latency compared to hosting it in South Korea.</p>",
